# Competency question: "What nodes are reachable from a start node within a
# bounded depth of 2 via a named step predicate?"  This exercises the headline
# capability of logic:PathShape: named, parametric, depth-bounded
# traversal that SPARQL §9 cannot express as a named/reusable construct.
#
# The fixture (path-traversal.ttl) supplies the chain:
#   ex:orgA --ex:linkedTo--> ex:orgB --ex:linkedTo--> ex:orgC
#                                                        --ex:linkedTo--> ex:orgD
#
# "Within 2 hops" is the bounded path logic:nearbyOrgs (pathStepPredicate
# ex:linkedTo, pathMinDepth 1, pathMaxDepth 2).  GMEOW's native projection lowers
# that shape to the EXTENDED SPARQL property path `ex:linkedTo{1,2}` — but a `{m,n}`
# quantifier is exactly the §9 construct standard SPARQL engines (oxigraph, which
# runs the competency cells) cannot parse.  So this query embeds the licensed
# STANDARD-SPARQL down-projection of that bounded path — the alternation
# `(ex:linkedTo|ex:linkedTo/ex:linkedTo)` — which is semantically identical for
# maxDepth 2 and actually executes on oxigraph.
#
# Anti-drift (CR7): the lossless extended-SPARQL projection
# `<...linkedTo>{1,2}` is pinned by the Rust golden
# `nearby_orgs_projection_emits_pinned_bounded_path`
# (crates/logic, projections/paths/tests.rs); if the projection drifts, that test
# fails.  ex:orgD is 3 hops away and must NOT appear (cqExactRows true).

PREFIX ex: <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/examples/logic/tests/>

SELECT ?neighbor WHERE {
    ex:orgA (ex:linkedTo|ex:linkedTo/ex:linkedTo) ?neighbor .
}
ORDER BY ?neighbor
